  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Participatory Water Monitoring</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project aims to promote the integral and transparent management of water resources in Aymara communities affected by climate change and contamination from mineral waste in the region of Puno, Peru.

Puno is one of the poorest regions of Peru, and it is increasingly affected by major socio-environmental conflict. 25% of Puno&#x2019;s land is under mining concession, and there are 257 registered mine tailings. In the last 15 years, the water available for farming and animal husbandry in this area has been severely contaminated by human and mine waste. Likewise, because the amount of rain in the area has diminished and the rainfall patterns are no longer the same, the rivers and lakes are drying &#x2013; up and the animals are forced to drink greater quantities of the contaminated water. As a direct consequence the size of the herds is steadily decreasing alongside families&#x2019; incomes. 

Suma Marka is a small NGO specialised in the preservation of biodiversity and participatory monitoring of environmental conditions in Lake Titicaca. Over the last nine months, Suma Marka has worked with communities, local, regional, and national decision-makers, as well as CAFOD partners in Bolivia and Peru, developing a simple yet very effective water monitoring scheme.       

By working with this new partner, the project will strengthen the technical capacity of community leaders, grassroots organisations and local authorities in the monitoring of environmental conditions and, in doing so, it will build the capacity of affected communities to enter into dialogue and negotiation with the local, regional and national government and private sector, using the information they have received from the project as a tool to lobby for more transparent and appropriate use of water resources.</narrative>
  </description>
